# PayMux verifies a checksum over each migration and refuses to start if an
# applied one changed underneath it. That check is byte-exact, so a file
# checked out with CRLF on one host and LF on another is, to PayMux, a
# different migration: the same commit would start on Linux and refuse to
# start on Windows.
#
# Normalising every text file to LF removes the possibility. It matters most
# for migrations, but a checksum is not the only thing that cares about bytes:
# webhook signatures are computed over raw payloads, and fixtures that drift
# by a line ending fail in ways that read as cryptographic problems.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.7z  binary
